WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.840
Scarring of the liver was once
thought to be an irreversible situation,

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.240
but a new and exciting field of experimental
drugs may put an end to this dogma.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.220
The liver is a large smooth organ that
carries out many essential functions.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:22.180
It stores and releases nutrients, aids digestion
and breaks down unwanted substances.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.560
Unfortunately, numerous chronic
diseases damage the liver

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.320
and when they continue for a long time they
cause scarring — a condition called fibrosis.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.740
The most common causes are
hepatitis viruses, long-term heavy drinking

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.680
and fatty liver disease associated
with obesity and diabetes.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.260
This scarring is caused by a
buildup of fibrous collagen proteins

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.620
which begin to replace healthy tissue.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:53.020
If the fibrosis keeps worsening, the liver can
progress to a state known as cirrhosis

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:59.600
At this stage, excessive scarring hampers the liver's
blood flow and stops it being able to do its job.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:06.880
Unchecked cirrhosis can lead directly to liver failure,
and puts patients at high risk for liver cancer.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.500
Cirrhosis currently kills a million
people worldwide each year.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.760
The good news is that the liver has its own
mechanisms that degrade scar tissue,

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.120
and this discovery has spurred a new wave of
research into developing drugs which can halt

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:26.180
the progression of fibrosis, or speed up healing
once the underlying disease has been treated.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:33.160
The best way to reduce fibrosis is to avoid it altogether by removing the risk factors associated with the disease,

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.140
but now scientists are examining
the biology of the scar-causing cells

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:40.880
as potential targets for therapeutic interventions

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.600
Scars are generated by cells called myofibroblasts.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.680
When these cells become activated,
they proliferate, migrate to injury sites

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.700
and secrete collagen into the extracellular matrix.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.740
As these collagen fibres accumulate,
they become cross-linked into a mesh

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.560
which stiffens the tissue and blocks its function.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:05.500
So researchers are aiming their sites,
first and foremost, at myofibroblasts.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:10.360
One strategy is to target the messenger
molecules which coordinate their activation.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:16.340
These are released by damaged liver cells and
immune cells and could targeted by new drugs.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:23.420
One example is a drug called Cenicriviroc that blocks receptors for profibrosis messengers called cytokines.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:29.460
Another set of drugs could inactivate myofibroblasts by targeting receptors on their surface called integrins

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.980
that allow them to interact with other
cells and the extracellular matrix.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:39.500
Multiple drugs that work this way are currently being explored and will enter human trials soon.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.820
Blocking the myofibroblasts' ability
to make collagen is another option.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:49.460
These drugs work by inserting small bits
of RNA into the nucleus of myofibroblasts,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.780
destabilising the machinery
that creates collagen fibres.

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:59.780
Another approach is to block the enzymes that
cross-link collagen fibres to stiffen the scar matrix.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.680
This makes the collagen easier for the body to degrade.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.800
Finally, an exciting new avenue
is the use of cell therapies.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.640
Introducing extra immune cells
called macrophages into rodents' livers

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.840
strongly boosts the organ's
intrinsic anti-scarring mechanisms

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.440
and now human trials using patient's
own macrophages have begun.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.540
All these therapies will require
clinical trials, and due to

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:27.860
the slow progressive nature of
liver fibrosis, this may take years.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:31.760
But new biomarkers in development
may speed up trials considerably

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.700
by showing whether the drugs are working, even
before the overall fibrosis starts to decline.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.340
Hopefully these new approaches will
help us to victory in the scar wars.

